I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the framework’s PXRD tooling first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best model through a GSAS-II refinement with reproducible outputs.

The chemistry narrows the plausible inventory to Sr–W–O phases plus residual SrCO₃/WO₃. I’m checking the supplied scientific environment and library index before peak-searching; this will also establish the exact GSAS-II interpreter and database paths.

The framework’s research-controller check is intentionally undeployed here, but the local diffraction stack and read-only structure library are separately available, so that does not block this requested sample analysis. I’m proceeding with local peak extraction and phase search only.

Peak matching already shows a dominant scheelite SrWO₄ phase and a distinct low-angle WO₃ triplet near 23–25° 2θ. Several weaker peaks remain, so I’m now testing the complete chemically allowed candidate set rather than assuming a two-phase product.

The dominant phase indexes consistently as tetragonal scheelite with approximately \(a=5.42\) Å and \(c=11.97\) Å after accounting for specimen/zero shift. I’m starting a staged GSAS-II refinement of that phase alone; its residual profile will make secondary-phase identification much less ambiguous.

The single-phase model leaves coherent, repeatable residual peaks—not just background mismatch—so at least one secondary crystalline phase is required. I’m screening every plausible Sr–W–O / precursor-derived COD model by its actual Rietveld improvement, with cell refinement enabled for modest reference mismatch.

Two candidates explain different parts of the residual: monoclinic WO₃ matches the 23–25° triplet, while the strongest 29.75° line is better explained by another phase. Some COD CIF settings import poorly in GSAS-II, so I’m standardizing only those candidate cells/settings for a fair screen before deciding whether the pattern is two- or three-phase.

The standardized models are now evaluating cleanly, but the large-cell reduced tungsten oxides are computationally heavier. Early fits favor monoclinic WO₃ for the low-angle triplet; I’m letting the remaining candidates finish so weak overlapping phases are not missed.

A physically meaningful Suortti surface-roughness correction drops Rwp from 20.24% to 18.53% and explains the suppressed low-angle scheelite intensities. The fitted parameters sit near the laboratory’s allowed roughness range, strengthening this interpretation; I’m now re-screening residual phases against that corrected profile.

After roughness correction, monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the 23–25° triplet, while a separate set of peaks at 14.99, 29.75, 30.89, 32.63, 35.32, and 44.67° remains. I’m screening that residual as a third phase; its repeated lines make it unlikely to be an artifact.

I’m using the known laboratory bounds on specimen displacement, angular zero, and the generator’s ≤0.5% reference strain as physical constraints—not merely choosing the lowest unconstrained Rwp. This calibration grid should distinguish a genuine third structure from a decoy that only fits after implausible lattice distortion.

The bounded profile refinement is now separating common instrument broadening from phase-specific crystallite size and microstrain. This is the main remaining source of the high Rwp; all nuisance parameters are being kept inside the laboratory’s stated physical ranges.

The profile jobs are still converging and are CPU-bound rather than stalled; the Sr₃W₂O₉ model is especially expensive because of its 56-site conventional cell. I’m retaining the full refinement because a shortcut here would bias both phase fractions and the phase comparison.

The bounded fit exposed a decisive problem with Sr₃W₂O₉: its crystallite size pins at the 0.04 µm lower bound while its strongest residual lines remain, so it is acting as a broad surrogate rather than explaining the phase. I’m removing it and refining the confirmed SrWO₄ + WO₃ background model before indexing the remaining phase independently.

The two confidently identified phases are scheelite SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃. I’m now isolating the remaining residual peaks and indexing them independently; the initially tempting Sr₃W₂O₉ match requires implausible lattice distortion, so I’m not treating it as established.

The residual pattern has now resolved into a coherent third fingerprint: its corrected lines at 14.63°, 17.68°, 29.40°, 30.54°, 33.76°, 34.97°, 44.34°, and 48.25° closely track COD 1520767. I’m checking the CIF’s actual chemistry and whether the apparent “SrC₂O₅” label represents a credible carbonate/oxalate phase or a database-formula artifact before accepting it.

The candidate is crystallographically compelling but chemically awkward: COD 1520767 is triclinic strontium oxalate hydrate (the deposited model omits D/H atom sites). I’m retaining it provisionally and testing whether a fully constrained three-phase refinement improves the whole pattern without forcing nonphysical profile or lattice parameters.

A useful correction from the laboratory rules: ambient H is an allowed source. That opens the companion COD structures that the first database screen missed, including tetragonal strontium oxalate dihydrate. I’m screening those now—the residual may be an H-bearing phase rather than the H-omitted triclinic model.

The decisive residual match is not the oxalate after all. COD 1532545 reproduces the strongest unexplained lines at 29.75°, 32.56°, 44.67°, 56.08°, and 50.55° with essentially no lattice rescaling. However, that COD deposition is malformed/incomplete: it is titled as a Sr–Mn–Cu oxide but contains only two partially occupied Sr sites. I’m testing it in GSAS-II as an empirical Sr-sublattice phase and will flag the chemistry as unreliable if it survives refinement.

The normalized Sr-sublattice model survives the stricter test: its cell refines only about +0.14% in \(a\) and −0.10% in \(c\), exactly the expected tolerance, and it lowers \(R_{wp}\) by about 1.4 percentage points on fixed-cell addition. I’m now finishing the physically bounded profile/coordinate refinement and quantifying how much of the remaining mismatch warrants another phase versus model incompleteness.

Before writing the deliverables, I’m replaying the saved project through the same zero-optimization GSAS-II path used by the framework scorer. This catches stale calculated arrays and verifies that the submitted CIFs, fractions, and full-grid profile all regenerate from `final.gpx`.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to robust SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ components, plus a third hexagonal fingerprint. I’m now validating that third model against the framework’s physical-structure checks; its closest database record is chemically incomplete, so I’m treating that identification and its fraction cautiously before finalizing the project.

The replay itself is sound (full-grid Rwp 13.81%), but the provisional third CIF contains two split Sr sites only 0.49 Å apart. That database entry could not have passed the generator’s registration rules, so I won’t submit it as a literal phase. I’m using its excellent lattice match as an indexing clue and searching for the physically valid Sr–W–O model that generated the same hexagonal reflections.

The third-phase indexing is reproducible, but ordinary SrWO₄-polymorph analogues do not reproduce its intensities. A physically separated repair of the two Sr split sites fits materially better than the malformed database record, so I’m refining that hypothesis while checking whether the remaining residuals require another phase or are profile/texture effects.

The physical split-site repair remains stable and improves the untextured refinement, but once the allowed [111] texture is included its fit is essentially tied with the simpler indexed model. I’m now testing preferred orientation of the two identified oxide phases, since the largest remaining residuals sit on their strongest overlapping peaks and can otherwise masquerade as an extra phase.

The Le Bail extraction provides the missing clue: the indexed phase has strong 100, 200, 310, 321, and 511 reflections that a Sr-only sublattice cancels. A companion COD record from the same publication contains the missing transition-metal/oxygen sublattice; merging its split sites and substituting W yields a chemically interpretable Sr₁.₃₂WO₃-type model. I’m testing that repaired composite directly against the extracted intensities.

The two conventional phases are secure: scheelite SrWO₄ is dominant and monoclinic WO₃ is minor. The remaining reflections index to a hexagonal cell near \(a=9.61\), \(c=3.93\) Å; I’m testing chemically complete oxide prototypes against its extracted intensities before freezing the final refinement.

A Fourier synthesis from the extracted hexagonal intensities gives a reproducible heavy-atom motif: a sixfold site at approximately \((1/3,0,1/4)\), plus weaker fourfold and twofold sites. I’m now checking whether assigning W/Sr to that motif improves the whole-pattern fit and yields physically reasonable interatomic distances; oxygen positions are much less constrained by Cu X-rays.

The apparent hexagonal symmetry was inflated by an incomplete database record; its published parent is actually trigonal \(P\bar{3}c1\). I’ve expanded the analogue search to all matching trigonal oxide prototypes, which is the most promising route to a chemically complete model for the third phase.

The trigonal search found a chemically coherent analogue family: a \(P\bar{3}c1\) WO₃-type model and a reduced Sr₂W₆O₁₈ bronze model both reproduce the indexed line positions and pass the geometry screen. Neither yet explains all intensities, so I’m broadening the prototype mapping before choosing whether the third phase can be named reliably.

The exact lattice match points to a misfit-composite analogue: the \(a\approx9.60\) Å Sr sublattice has a complementary W–O subsystem with its own \(c\approx2.60\) Å repeat. I’m testing those two subsystems separately, since forcing both into the 3.93 Å cell was crystallographically incorrect and could explain the remaining unmatched reflections.

The complementary-subsystem test did not refine to a meaningful scale, so there is no evidence for a separate fourth crystalline phase. I’m finalizing the three-phase model with the hexagonal Sr₂W₆O₁₈-type assignment explicitly marked provisional; the scheelite SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ assignments are secure, while the hexagonal oxygen sublattice and its fraction remain model-dependent.

I’m doing one last physical-geometry check on the tentative hexagonal phase before locking the project. The key issue is avoiding an apparently good profile fit that requires implausibly short Sr–Sr contacts; if the chemically complete WO₃ analogue is sound enough, I’ll use that more conservative model and quantify its model dependence.

The geometry check found a better conservative description for the third lattice: a P6₃/mcm WO₃ average framework built directly from the indexed heavy-atom sublattice. It gives six-coordinate W with 1.90–1.96 Å W–O bonds and no unphysical cation contacts. I’m replacing the earlier Sr-bearing proxy with this model; its identity will remain explicitly tentative because the powder intensities do not uniquely determine the oxygen topology.

The idealized WO₃ network now gives a reproducible fresh-forward Rwp of 13.29%, better than the earlier proxy, but its phase scale is strongly coupled to a March–Dollase term (0.30). I’m checking the single symmetry-preserving W/O positional degree of freedom rather than accepting unconstrained coordinate refinement, which immediately drove oxygen sites into nonphysical geometry.

The framework’s laboratory prior limits March–Dollase ratios to 0.7–1.4, so I’m rejecting the numerically better 0.28 solution as an out-of-scope texture overfit. I’m testing a physically interpretable alternative within that limit: the same WO₃ network with the W orbit represented as two mutually exclusive half-occupied split positions, which is what the peak intensities indicate and avoids treating both close sites as simultaneously occupied atoms.

The constrained model is now selected. It retains the laboratory’s texture bound (March–Dollase 0.70), gives a fresh atomic forward-replay Rwp of 14.54%, and assigns 78.03% SrWO₄, 11.74% monoclinic WO₃, and a nominal 10.22% to the tentative hexagonal phase. I’m staging the replay-exported CIFs and documenting that the third phase’s structure and fraction are model-dependent.

A final validity audit flagged the split-W representation for sub-0.8 Å partial-occupancy contacts. Those contacts are interpretable as correlated disorder, but the CIF cannot encode that correlation, and the fit gain is only 0.06 Rwp. I’m therefore favoring the contact-free symmetry-averaged WO₃ framework for the submitted model and will carry the disorder interpretation only as uncertainty in the report.

The deliverables are written. I’m running the final consistency pass now: exact CIF/project correspondence, normalized fractions, full-grid profile columns, and a fresh replay from the staged `final.gpx`.

The final replay is internally consistent. One remaining scientific caveat surfaced in the monoclinic reference model: its rounded oxygen coordinates create four 1.58 Å O–O contacts. I’m checking equivalent WO₃ references or a geometry-regularized coordinate set; the monoclinic phase identification is secure, but I don’t want to submit avoidable oxygen-coordinate artifacts.

A geometry-valid monoclinic WO₃ reference with the same refined lattice fits essentially identically (Rwp changes by only 0.024 percentage point) and removes the 1.58 Å O–O artifact; its shortest contact is 1.715 Å. I’m adopting that model. This changes the normalized fractions only slightly, to 79.13% SrWO₄, 11.65% monoclinic WO₃, and 9.22% tentative hexagonal phase.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- SrWO₄ (scheelite): 79.13 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 11.65 wt%
- Tentative hexagonal P6₃/mcm average-model phase: 9.22 wt%

Fresh GSAS-II replay: Rwp 14.630%, Rp 9.751%, GOF 4.142. The hexagonal phase’s structure and fraction remain model-dependent; plausible abundance is roughly 9–15 wt%.

